openmedia.library/OM_CodecName                 openmedia.library/OM_CodecName

   NAME
	OM_CodecName -- a codec's name, for people (V1)

   SYNOPSIS
	name = OM_CodecName(codec)
	D0                  D0

	CONST_STRPTR OM_CodecName(ULONG codec);

   FUNCTION
	Gives the English name of an OMC_ code: "H.264" for OMC_H264,
	"AAC" for OMC_AAC. The string is the library's and stays while the
	library is open.

   INPUTS
	codec - an OMC_ code from <openmedia/openmedia.h>.

   RESULT
	name - the name, or NULL for OMC_NONE, OMC_OTHER and codes this
	    version doesn't know. For OMC_OTHER, omi_VideoCodecName and
	    omi_AudioCodecName hold the file's own code for the codec.

   SEE ALSO
	OM_ObtainMediaInfoA(), <openmedia/openmedia.h>

openmedia.library/OM_ObtainMediaInfoA   openmedia.library/OM_ObtainMediaInfoA

   NAME
	OM_ObtainMediaInfoA -- what a file is (V1)
	OM_ObtainMediaInfo -- varargs stub for OM_ObtainMediaInfoA

   SYNOPSIS
	info = OM_ObtainMediaInfoA(tags)
	D0                         A0

	struct OMMediaInfo *OM_ObtainMediaInfoA(struct TagItem *tags);

	info = OM_ObtainMediaInfo(Tag tag1, ...)

   FUNCTION
	Reads the headers of a file and says what it holds: a picture, a
	sound, a video or an animation; its container and format; its codecs;
	its size in pixels and depth, its length, frame rate and frames; its
	channels, sample rate and bits; its bit rate; and how many tracks of
	each kind it has.

	The library's own parsers know MP4, QuickTime and 3GP, Matroska and
	WebM, AVI, MPEG program and transport streams, Ogg (Theora, Vorbis,
	Opus, FLAC, Speex), MPEG-1/2, H.264 and HEVC streams, WAV, AIFF and
	AIFF-C, IFF 8SVX, 16SV, ANIM and ILBM, FLAC, MP3 and the other MPEG
	audio layers, AAC in ADTS, PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WebP, HEIF and AVIF.
	They read only the header bytes they need (for some formats the end of
	the file too, for its length), and never more than OMA_MaxRead bytes
	in all. A damaged file gives what could be read, never a hang.

	A file they don't know goes to datatypes.library (ObtainDataType):
	its group gives omi_Kind, and the datatype's name omi_FormatName and
	omi_DataType. Pictures are asked too, so omi_DataType names the
	datatype that shows them.

	The call runs on the caller's stack and needs about 1.5 KB of it;
	with OMA_Decode the datatype's own needs come on top. It allocates
	about 112 KB while it runs.

   INPUTS
	tags - one of these says which file:

	OMA_Name (CONST_STRPTR) - the file's name.

	OMA_Lock (BPTR) - a shared lock on the file. The lock stays the
	    caller's: the call neither keeps nor unlocks it.

	OMA_FileHandle (BPTR) - a file the caller has open. It stays open,
	    and its position is put back before the call returns.

	and these change what it does:

	OMA_DataTypes (BOOL) - ask datatypes.library about what the parsers
	    don't know, and name a picture's datatype. Default TRUE.

	OMA_Decode (BOOL) - let a datatype load the whole file when that is
	    the only way to learn a picture's size and depth, or the length of
	    a sound or animation the parsers don't know. Default FALSE.
	    THIS IS A FULL DECODE: it costs as much as showing the file (a
	    few seconds for a large JPEG on a 68040, more on a 68030), and the
	    picture's or sample's memory while it runs. The parsers never
	    need it for the formats listed above. omi_Flags has
	    OMIF_DECODED when a datatype was asked to load the file.

	OMA_MaxRead (ULONG) - the most bytes the parsers read from the file.
	    Default 1 MB; anything under 64 KB counts as 64 KB.

	OMA_Error (LONG *) - gets OMERR_OK, or why the result is NULL:
	    OMERR_NOFILE (the file couldn't be opened; IoErr() says why),
	    OMERR_NOMEM, OMERR_READ, OMERR_ARGS (no file given).

   RESULT
	info - a struct OMMediaInfo (<openmedia/openmedia.h>), read-only,
	    to be given back with OM_ReleaseMediaInfo(); or NULL. A file
	    nobody knows is not an error: omi_Kind is OMK_UNKNOWN and
	    omi_FormatName "Unknown". A value not known is 0, a name not
	    known NULL; omi_FormatName is never NULL.

	The library allocates the structure, so later versions add fields
	at its end: check omi_Version (or omi_Size) before reading a field
	newer than version 1.

   EXAMPLE
	struct OMMediaInfo *mi;
	LONG err;

	if ((mi = OM_ObtainMediaInfo(OMA_Name, (ULONG)"Work:Film.mp4",
	                             OMA_Error, (ULONG)&err, TAG_DONE))) {
	    Printf("%s, %lu x %lu, %lu ms\n", (ULONG)mi->omi_FormatName,
	           mi->omi_Width, mi->omi_Height, mi->omi_Duration);
	    OM_ReleaseMediaInfo(mi);
	}

   NOTES
	The parsers follow the formats' public specifications. They read
	what a file holds, never what it is called: a name's extension is
	not looked at.

	A length marked OMIF_DURATION_ESTIMATED was worked out from the bit
	rate (an MP3 with no Xing or VBRI header, an ADTS stream).

   BUGS
	Files over 2 GB are read only as far as AmigaDOS can seek.

   SEE ALSO
	OM_ReleaseMediaInfo(), OM_CodecName(), datatypes.library/ObtainDataType(),
	<openmedia/openmedia.h>, C:OpenMediaInfo

openmedia.library/OM_ReleaseMediaInfo   openmedia.library/OM_ReleaseMediaInfo

   NAME
	OM_ReleaseMediaInfo -- give back what OM_ObtainMediaInfoA gave (V1)

   SYNOPSIS
	OM_ReleaseMediaInfo(info)
	                    A0

	VOID OM_ReleaseMediaInfo(struct OMMediaInfo *info);

   FUNCTION
	Frees the structure and the names it points to.

   INPUTS
	info - from OM_ObtainMediaInfoA(), or NULL (nothing is done).

   SEE ALSO
	OM_ObtainMediaInfoA()

